#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221096 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "fe6b394f-a130-5746-a5c9-0fbbee020554" :question/math-academy-id "e-21424" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2}$, find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}$." :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-5t} \\cdot t\\sin (2t)} &= (2(2)(s + 5))/(((s + 5)^{2} + 2^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4(s + 5))/(((s + 5)^{2} + 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221100 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeb2589b-0135-5bbb-bc91-ec121ee5defd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "04233a8a-069f-5649-b198-c6bbf5b96742" :question/math-academy-id "e-21531" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$ for $s>s_0$. Suppose we wish to find $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-7t} f(t)\\}$. Show the steps to compute this transform." :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-7t}f(t)} &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st} \\cdot e^{-7t}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{- (s + 7)t}f(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $s > s_0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7)\n$$\n\nwhere\n$s + 7 > s_0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad s > s_0 - 7$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221103 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "1e5e6e3a-306b-5e8c-a8ba-baa0d63a1813" :question/math-academy-id "e-21549" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$. How can we obtain the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)$?" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by $e^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a > 0$, the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n- If $a < 0$, the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$, we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221108 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/id #uuid "05394517-ee39-5b77-9f84-b439d907dd3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21590" :question/problem "Given that\n$L{t^{n}}|= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}, \\mid s > 0$,\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4$." :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= L{2t^{2} + 5te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= L{2t^{2}} + L{5te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= 2L{t^{2}} + 5L{te^{4t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{t^{n}} &= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{t} &= \\frac{1}{s^{2}} \\\\\nL{t^{2}} &= \\frac{2}{s^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= 2L{t^{2}} + 5L{te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{s^{3}} + 5 \\cdot (1)/((s - 4)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{s^{3}} + (5)/((s - 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8379fe97-9f04-5bce-a3e3-05b850e633c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6)/((s - 3)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220085} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f5d9e1-7fe7-56c7-a9f5-019bb18a411b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/((s + 4)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220086} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e618ff-bd63-543b-9f45-c936a764c677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6)/((s - 4)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220087} {:answer/id #uuid "0a5fa711-7c73-56de-a1f7-dae90a97fa7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6)/((s + 3)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220088} {:answer/id #uuid "769503d5-a490-5d08-b8e8-8216d7fe1c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/((s - 4)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e618ff-bd63-543b-9f45-c936a764c677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6)/((s - 4)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220087} :answer-field/id #uuid "d130ee1f-de7b-59e9-a35f-fc2653537443" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220084}] :question/id #uuid "bc424099-23a8-593e-8e7c-1170c0265f21" :question/math-academy-id "q-325858" :question/problem "Given that $L{t^{n}} = \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}$, find the Laplace transform $L{e^{4t} \\cdot t^{3}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d78e6349-4b76-5fc8-a8af-75e323792c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $3$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220103} {:answer/id #uuid "df8a16e5-7fe8-541e-8dc9-6b6d9994d52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220104} {:answer/id #uuid "fec67f3b-7782-5c27-8e31-05591f00fceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $3$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220105} {:answer/id #uuid "124b32a3-7318-5b26-8b3c-d61fe7b198b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220106} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa706a2-032a-547f-9c50-61a232cd434e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "124b32a3-7318-5b26-8b3c-d61fe7b198b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220106} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d927622-ce5f-5984-a890-37d6b5964010" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220102}] :question/id #uuid "5b93a8ba-b183-509b-a5e3-8df4ea93eca1" :question/math-academy-id "q-328557" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)} = F(s)$. How can we obtain the graph of $L{e^{3t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeb2589b-0135-5bbb-bc91-ec121ee5defd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9755646-085f-57d7-b579-1853a0799c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{− (s + 2)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220109} {:answer/id #uuid "aede3730-b686-5693-88ba-68e5c587f39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{(s − 2)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220110} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c93dc2-8dad-5627-b74a-1eaddabe7845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{(s + 2)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220111} {:answer/id #uuid "5bef6b99-6c97-566b-9f3b-6c4d11f14fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{− (s − 2)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220112}] :answer-field/id #uuid "50f656c0-6876-5ac1-9f42-d471c6fbcec6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220108} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8f5dc76-c0ae-5b49-9f18-632fe8321cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{st} ⋅ e^{2t}f(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220114} {:answer/id #uuid "eb14d6ca-be80-5749-9fd0-26a3a8c1432c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{− st} ⋅ e^{2t}f(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220115} {:answer/id #uuid "f429e119-2d3b-586e-82e3-dbae2ca14c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{− st} ⋅ e^{2}f(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220116} {:answer/id #uuid "023e6b39-27f7-5ae5-97bc-03fc2712bffa" :answer/type {:db/iden